  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe anomalous ridge axis is located near or just west of the \nMississippi this morning. It will become centered over Ark-La-Tex \nbefore further weakening and suppression as we get later into the \nweekend. What this means for our area is we will return to a \ndiurnally induced precipitation pattern under weak northwesterly \nflow. There will be a chance of isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms during the afternoon and early evening hours the entirety of \nthe forecast period. Like usual, chances will be best over the \nhigher terrain of the Southern Appalachians, Cumberland Plateau and \nMountains. No severe weather expected; the only hazards will be \nlightning, possible heavy rain, and wind gusts. As we approach the \nmiddle of the week next week, the upper level pattern may bring \nshortwave troughing close to the region possibly providing more \nwidespread precipitation.\n\nTemperature-wise, today appears will be the final \"hottest\" area-\nwide with mid to upper 90s from the central valley down to the GA \nborder. Heat indices could reach the lower 100s range. Tomorrow, \ntemperatures and heat indices will be a smidge less for some, mostly \nnortheastern Tennessee into southwest Virginia. A touch less in \ntemperatures and heat indices Sunday and Monday, however, the more \nnotable drop possibly comes Tuesday. An approaching frontal system \nand slightly lower heights aloft will be the big reason for what \nmight be noticeable relief early next week. Then, the relief further \ninfiltrates with lower temperatures and dew points Wednesday and \nThursday. Some valley locations may see upper 70s to lower 80s for \nhighs, which would be more in line climatologically for the end of \nSeptember. Until then, Chattanooga will continue to build onto their \nrecord streak of 90 degrees or higher, now sitting at 45 days. Also \ncannot go without mentioning that even though most of the area won't \nreach Heat Advisory criteria the next couple of days, that doesn't \nmean to let your guard down when it comes to being outdoors, staying \ncool, and hydrated.",
